---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why I started LightPhotos — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A photo shoot with friends, a Lightroom license that stopped working, and an afternoon spent building the small photo editor I actually needed."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blog</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-23">June 23, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why I started LightPhotos</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A Lightroom license that stopped working, a few apps that didn't fit,
          and an afternoon spent building the small photo editor I actually needed.
        </p>
      </header>

      <h2>A card full of RAW files</h2>
      <p>
        I recently took some pictures with my Sony full-frame camera. I always
        shoot RAW, so most photos needed some adjustment before I could share
        them.
      </p>

      <h2>Software I paid for and can't use</h2>
      <p>
        Years ago I paid close to $200 for a Lightroom Classic license. I
        thought I'd bought it for good. Then I upgraded macOS, and starting
        around Monterey, the installer stopped running. I still own the
        license. I just can't use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I had also bought Luminar AI. It was OK, but it didn't quite match how
        I work. I wanted something with a lot of keyboard shortcuts.
      </p>

      <h2>The years of photos I lost</h2>
      <p>
        I like what Apple Photos can do, but I won't trust it with my photos
        again.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your whole library is stored as a single file, in a format only Apple
        Photos can read. I found out what that means the hard way. I lost
        several years of photos because a newer version of macOS refused to
        open Photos libraries from my older backups. I had backed them up and
        the backups were still there, but the only app that could read them
        wouldn't open them.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also doesn't fit how I store photos. I keep my main drive for recent
        work and put older shoots on other drives. I don't want hundreds of
        gigabytes of old photos on my main drive, locked in a library I might
        not be able to open in a few years.
      </p>

      <h2>Too much to learn</h2>
      <p>
        Next I tried Darktable and a few other free editors. Every time, I
        opened the app and faced a wall of panels, modules and settings I
        didn't recognize. I didn't want to learn a new craft. I wanted to make
        some photos a little brighter and send them to my friends.
      </p>

      <h2>Another subscription</h2>
      <p>
        That left the obvious option: pay Adobe about $10 a month for Lightroom
        again. It isn't the money. I edit photos in bursts: a trip, an event,
        an afternoon with friends. Then months can go by before I open an editor
        again. I didn't want to pay every month for something I use a few
        times a year, especially after already paying for Lightroom once.
      </p>

      <h2>So I built one</h2>
      <p>
        That afternoon, I sat down to build a basic version myself. I didn't
        want to clone Lightroom. I wanted the smallest tool that would get
        these photos to my friends. It had to do seven things:
      </p>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Open any folder and show the photos in it.</strong> No
          import step and no library file. My photos stay as ordinary files on
          whatever drive holds them, so I can always open them with something
          else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rate photos from 1 to 5,</strong> so I can go through a
          shoot quickly and mark the keepers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Filter by rating,</strong> so I only see the photos I like.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adjust exposure, highlights, shadows and the other basics.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep every adjustment non-destructive.</strong> The original
          file is never touched, so I can always undo an edit or start over.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Export selected photos to JPEG,</strong> ready to send.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run on a Mac.</strong>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That's all it had to do. Anything that wasn't on the list didn't make
        it into the first version.
      </p>

      <h2>My learnings</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Keep photos as plain files.</strong> A backup only helps if
          something can still open it. Ordinary files on a drive can be opened
          by any app, today or years from now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Buying software once doesn't mean owning it forever.</strong>
          An OS upgrade can end a license just as surely as a cancelled
          subscription.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>More features isn't better.</strong> The free editors could
          do everything, and that's what made them hard to use.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the list first.</strong> Deciding on seven things up
          front made it easy to say no to everything else.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        In a future post, I'll go through the architectural choices behind
        LightPhotos and the pros and cons of each.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
